        "language": "en",
        "bound_author_id": 1050,
        "text": "John Stuart Mill examines when society and government may restrict a person's freedom. He argues that preventing harm to others can justify intervention, while disagreement or disapproval alone cannot. The essay considers freedom of thought and discussion, individual ways of living, and the pressure exerted by public opinion. Rather than telling a fictional story, it develops an argument about the limits of authority and the value of allowing different views to be heard.",
